#X obj 60 638 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Inlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 666 1 float: note number. Velocity is taken from inlet 2 \;   and velocity 0 is a note off. \; 1 list: note \, velocity and \, if you like \, the MIDI channel. \; 1 anything: a message \, see Messages. \; 2 float: velocity for the next note \, 0 to 127 (default 100). \; 3 float or list: raw MIDI bytes \, as midiin gives them. \; 3 anything: a message \, the same set as inlet 1., f 64;
#X obj 60 792 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Outlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 820 1 signal: left output. \; 2 signal: right output. \; 3 anything: info \, name \, 0-127 and value text for every \;   change \, the patch that plays \, its name \, the channels. \; 3 display title value: two lines for a display: a control \;   set by a message (VCF freq \, 64) \, the patch that plays \;   (Preset 12 \, Harpsichord 1) \, a store (Stored \, User 11). \; 3 float: SysEx bytes for midiout., f 64;
#X obj 60 962 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Messages 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 990 name v: set a control \, in the values the panel shows. \; name: report it as info. \; n name v: set it from a pot or a MIDI CC \, 0 to 127. \; ninfo name: report it as info. \; preset n: a factory preset \, read only. \; user n: a slot of the user bank \, 0 to 55. \; init: a plain sawtooth patch to start from. \; reload: the same patch again \, edits dropped. \; store n: the playing patch into user slot n \, in memory. \;   Without n: the user slot chosen or stored last. \; presetstep 1 or -1 \, userstep 1 or -1: the next or previous \;   factory preset or user slot \, from the one chosen last. \; save: store \, then userwrite (the pads' save). \; cycle name [-1]: the next (previous) value \, round and round. \; userwrite: write the user bank to junouser-bank.syx. \; userread: read it again. Both also take a file name. \; usersend: the user bank as SysEx on outlet 3. \; presets \, users: list the names. \; bend f: pitch wheel \, -1 to 1 \, times bend. \; mod f: modulation wheel \, 0 to 1: vibrato. \; sustain 0/1: hold pedal. \; channel n: MIDI receive channel \, 0 is all. \; txchannel n: channel of the controller feedback and of \;   SysEx \, 1 to 16. \; sysexout 0/1: send SysEx for every change \, see SysEx. \; thru 0/1: MIDI thru \, on by default. MIDI from inlet 3 \;   leaves outlet 3 unchanged \, notes from inlet 1 go out \;   on txchannel. SysEx arriving is passed on as well. \; status: report patch \, channels and sounding voices. \; stop: all notes off. \; reset: all notes off at once \, chorus emptied., f 64;
#X obj 60 1500 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty MIDI 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1528 midiin: raw bytes into inlet 3. juno~ takes notes with \;   velocity \, bend \, program change \, CC 1 (vibrato) \, 7 \;   (volume) \, 64 (hold) and Juno-106 SysEx. \; midiout: the floats of outlet 3 \, SysEx patches and dumps. \; junocc 1: the CC pages from 20 on \, on channel 1. \; junopads 10 \, lkdisplay: the Launchkey (see Launchkey). \; channel 1 at load: juno~ plays channel 1 only \, so the pads \;   on channel 10 do not play notes. \; Without midiin: send note lists and bend \, mod \, sustain., f 64;
#X obj 60 1686 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controls 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1714 cutoff (patch): cutoff of the low-pass filter. \; res (patch): resonance \, up to self-oscillation. \; env (patch): envelope to cutoff. \; attack (patch): envelope attack. \; decay (patch): envelope decay. \; sustain (patch): envelope sustain level. \; release (patch): envelope release. \; chorus (patch): chorus. I+II is the Juno-60's third mode \, a Juno-106 plays it as I. Values: 0 off \, 1 I \, 2 II \, 3 I+II., f 64;
#X obj 60 1872 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controller\ layer\ and\ editor 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1900 junocc is the controller layer: MIDI CC from 20 on in pages of 8 \, the same pages as for pdoly8~ \, tx81z~ and d50~. Page 1 is filter and envelope \, page k is row k of the editor. It turns a CC into an n message and sends every change made elsewhere back to the controller as CC. Its argument is the MIDI channel \, and it follows the channels juno~ reports. \; junoedit is the editor: click it in the object area above. All 33 controls in rows of 8 \, the factory presets and the user bank \, the MIDI channels and SysEx. It explains itself in its readme. \; All names are listed at the end of this patch with their CC numbers., f 64;
#X obj 60 2106 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Launchkey 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2134 Launchkey: the Novation Launchkey Mini MK4 with the custom firmware maltepeter2000/launchkey_firmware \, set up the same for every instrument of bela-workstation (launchkey/README.md there). \; junocc 1: the controller layer. Encoders in mode Custom 1 send CC pages from 20 in rows of 8 on the keys channel \, every change made elsewhere goes back to them on the send channel (txchannel of juno~) \, CC 3 asks for all values. \; junopads 10: the pads in Drum mode (notes 36-51 on channel 10 \, the Drums Channel): top row 36 factory + \, 37 user + \, 38 init (press twice within a second) \, 39-43 own \, bottom row 44 factory - \, 45 user - \, 46 save \, 47-51 own. Own pads of juno~: 39 chorus I \, II \, I+II \, off (round and round) \, 47 Juno-106 / Juno-60. \; Pad colours \, sent by junopads itself with noteout on channel 10 (velocity = colour + 32 x mode): factory blue \, user green \, init orange (flashing while the second press is awaited) \, save red (white for a moment when pressed) \, own pads purple \, free pads off. They go out at load \, on leds \, and when the Launchkey asks for them (CC 3 = 127 on channel 10 \, sent when Drum mode is selected). \; lkdisplay: the display lines (display <title> <value>: the control you turn \, Preset \, User \, Init or Stored with the patch name) as SysEx for the Launchkey's display -> midiout., f 64;
#X obj 60 2532 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty The\ instrument 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2560 The engine is a Juno-60: one DCO per voice with sawtooth \, pulse \, sub oscillator and noise \, the four-position high-pass \, the IR3109 low-pass \, one envelope \, one LFO and the chorus with its two bucket-brigade lines. Six voices \, handed out in turn as on the instrument. \; A patch is kept in the format of the Juno-106 \, whose panel is nearly the same and which has MIDI: 16 sliders from 0 to 127 and 8 switches. So Juno-106 patch collections play \, and patches go to a Juno-106 and back. Of the Juno-60's own extras \, chorus I+II is kept (a Juno-106 plays it as I) and pulse width from the envelope is left out. The arpeggiator is left out as well. \; Not on the instrument: velocity \, off until vellevel or velcutoff is raised \, and the modulation wheel \, which adds vibrato as on a Juno-106. The oscillator and the filter run at twice the sample rate., f 64;
#X obj 60 2830 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Presets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 460 2882 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 2858 4 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 2882 preset \$1;
#X floatatom 160 2858 4 0 55 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 2882 user \$1;
#X obj 60 2918 r \$0-juno-out;
#X obj 60 2942 route preset user;
#X obj 60 2968 unpack f s;
#X floatatom 60 2996 4 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X symbolatom 100 2996 20 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 260 2968 unpack f s;
#X floatatom 260 2996 4 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X symbolatom 300 2996 20 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 82 3024 preset selects a factory preset \, user a slot of the user bank. The third outlet answers with the number and the name. \; store 5 followed by userwrite keeps the playing patch in user slot 5 and on disk. The editor does both with save patch. \; The controls of the SETUP row \, volume to velcutoff \, belong to the instrument. A patch change leaves them alone and they are not stored., f 64;
#X obj 60 3150 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty DCO 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 460 3202 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 3178 6 0 2 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 3202 range \$1;
#X floatatom 160 3178 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 3202 pulse \$1;
#X floatatom 260 3178 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 260 3202 saw \$1;
#X floatatom 360 3178 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 360 3202 sub \$1;
#X text 82 3232 range (patch): octave of the oscillator. Values: 0 16' \, 1 8' \, 2 4'. \; pulse (patch): pulse wave. Values: 0 off \, 1 on. \; saw (patch): sawtooth wave. Values: 0 off \, 1 on. \; sub (patch): level of the sub oscillator \, one octave down., f 64;
#X obj 460 3340 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 3316 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 3340 noise \$1;
#X floatatom 160 3316 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 3340 pwm \$1;
#X floatatom 260 3316 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 260 3340 pwmmode \$1;
#X floatatom 360 3316 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 360 3340 dcolfo \$1;
#X text 82 3370 noise (patch): noise level. \; pwm (patch): pulse width: the depth of the LFO \, or the width itself with pwmmode MAN. \; pwmmode (patch): pulse width from the LFO or fixed. Values: 0 LFO \, 1 MAN. \; dcolfo (patch): LFO to pitch (vibrato)., f 64;
#X obj 60 3480 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty LFO\ VCF\ VCA 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 460 3532 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 3508 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 3532 lforate \$1;
#X floatatom 160 3508 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 3532 lfodelay \$1;
#X floatatom 260 3508 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 260 3532 vcflfo \$1;
#X floatatom 360 3508 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 360 3532 kybd \$1;
#X text 82 3562 lforate (patch): LFO rate \, 0.3 to 20 Hz. \; lfodelay (patch): time until the LFO sets in after the first key of a phrase. \; vcflfo (patch): LFO to cutoff. \; kybd (patch): keyboard follow of the cutoff., f 64;
#X obj 460 3670 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 3646 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 3670 envpol \$1;
#X floatatom 160 3646 6 0 3 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 3670 hpf \$1;
#X floatatom 260 3646 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 260 3670 level \$1;
#X floatatom 360 3646 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 360 3670 vcamode \$1;
#X text 82 3700 envpol (patch): polarity of the envelope on the cutoff. Values: 0 pos \, 1 neg. \; hpf (patch): high-pass filter \, four positions. Values: 0 0 \, 1 1 \, 2 2 \, 3 3. \; level (patch): level of the patch \, in front of the chorus. \; vcamode (patch): the amplifier follows the envelope or the gate. Values: 0 ENV \, 1 GATE., f 64;
#X obj 60 3826 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Setup 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3854 These belong to the instrument and are not stored in a patch., f 64;
#X obj 460 3898 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 3874 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 3898 volume \$1;
#X floatatom 160 3874 6 -50 50 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 3898 tune \$1;
#X floatatom 260 3874 6 0 7 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 260 3898 bend \$1;
#X floatatom 360 3874 6 -2 2 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 360 3898 octave \$1;
#X text 82 3928 volume (127): master volume \, after the chorus. \; tune (0): master tune in cents. \; bend (2): bender range in semitones. \; octave (0): octave transposition of the keyboard., f 64;
#X obj 460 4020 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 3996 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 4020 vellevel \$1;
#X floatatom 160 3996 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 4020 velcutoff \$1;
#X floatatom 260 3996 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 260 4020 model \$1;
#X text 82 4050 vellevel (0): velocity to level. 0 is the instrument \, which has no velocity. \; velcutoff (0): velocity to cutoff \, a soft key is darker. \; model (0): which Juno the patch plays on: the Juno-60 \, or the Juno-106 as measured on Cherry Audio's DCO-106 (cutoff and resonance \, contour times and depth \, LFO \, high-pass \, chorus \, sub and noise levels \, pulse width \, VCA level). Values: 0 60 \, 1 106., f 64;
#X obj 60 4192 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Voices 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4220 These belong to the instrument and are not stored in a patch., f 64;
#X obj 460 4264 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 4240 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 4264 calibrate \$1;
#X floatatom 160 4240 6 0 127 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 4264 chorusnoise \$1;
#X text 82 4294 calibrate (0): how far the six voices drift apart \, as on an instrument out of calibration: each voice its own tuning \, cutoff \, resonance and level. 0 is six identical voices. \; chorusnoise (0): hiss of the chorus lines \, heard while the chorus is on. 0 is none., f 64;
#X obj 60 4388 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Playing 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 460 4440 s \$0-juno;
#X floatatom 60 4416 6 -1 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 60 4440 bend \$1;
#X floatatom 160 4416 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 160 4440 mod \$1;
#X floatatom 260 4416 6 0 1 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 260 4440 sustain \$1;
#X text 82 4470 bend (0): pitch wheel \, -1 to 1. The range in semitones is the control bend. \; mod (0): modulation wheel \, 0 to 1. Adds vibrato at the rate of the LFO \, without its delay. \; sustain (0): hold pedal. Notes released while it is 1 sound until it returns to 0., f 64;
#X obj 60 4580 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty By\ address 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 460 4608 s \$0-juno;
#X msg 60 4608 param 5 60;
#X msg 190 4608 param 5;
#X msg 320 4608 dump;
#X msg 60 4642 sysexdump;
#X text 82 4676 param a v: sets one of the 18 bytes of the patch \, as a Juno-106 counts them: 0 to 15 are the sliders in the order LFO rate \, LFO delay \, DCO LFO \, PWM \, noise \, VCF frequency \, resonance \, envelope \, LFO \, keyboard \, VCA level \, attack \, decay \, sustain \, release \, sub level. 16 and 17 hold the switches. param a reports the byte. \; dump: reports every control as info. \; sysexdump: sends the playing patch as SysEx \, see below., f 64;
#X obj 60 4818 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty SysEx 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 4846 The object speaks the exclusive format of the Juno-106. The bytes arrive with the rest of the MIDI at the third inlet and leave the third outlet as floats. Messages on another channel than the receive channel are ignored. \; A patch message (F0 41 30 channel number \, 18 bytes \, F7) or a manual message (31) replaces the playing patch \, as on a Juno-106. A parameter message (F0 41 32 channel parameter value F7) changes one of its 18 bytes. \; sysexdump sends the playing patch and usersend the 56 patches of the user bank \, numbered 0 to 55. A Juno-106 plays what it receives but does not store it by itself \, so after usersend it holds the last patch. Its memory is written from its own panel. \; sysexout 1 makes the object behave like a Juno-106: every edit leaves as a parameter message and every patch change as a patch message. What came in as SysEx is not sent back. \; Switch bytes. 16: bits 0 to 2 are 16' \, 8' \, 4' \, bit 3 pulse \, bit 4 sawtooth \, bit 5 chorus off \, bit 6 chorus I \, else II. 17: bit 0 PWM manual \, bit 1 envelope negative \, bit 2 VCA gate \, bits 3 and 4 the high-pass \, 3 for position 0 down to 0 for position 3. Bit 5 of byte 17 is the object's own: chorus I+II., f 64;
#X obj 60 5196 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Data\ files 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 5224 The object needs no files. The 56 factory sounds of the Juno-60 are built in \, presets 0 to 55. \; Every .syx file in the folder of the external is read as a bank of Juno-106 patches and joins the factory presets \, in alphabetical order \, from preset 56 on. They are named after the file and the place a Juno-106 shows \, A11 to B88. \; junouser-bank.syx in the same folder is the user bank: 56 patch messages of 24 bytes. Until it has been written the user bank holds 56 init patches. Its slots are shown as on the Juno-60 \, bank and number \, 11 to 78., f 64;
#X obj 60 5398 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty All\ controls\ and\ their\ CC\ numbers 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 5426 cutoff 0..127 \, CC 20: cutoff of the low-pass filter \; res 0..127 \, CC 21: resonance \, up to self-oscillation \; env 0..127 \, CC 22: envelope to cutoff \; attack 0..127 \, CC 23: envelope attack \; decay 0..127 \, CC 24: envelope decay \; sustain 0..127 \, CC 25: envelope sustain level \; release 0..127 \, CC 26: envelope release \; chorus 0..3 \, CC 27: chorus. I+II is the Juno-60's third mode \, \;   a Juno-106 plays it as I \; range 0..2 \, CC 28: octave of the oscillator \; pulse 0..1 \, CC 29: pulse wave \; saw 0..1 \, CC 30: sawtooth wave \; sub 0..127 \, CC 31: level of the sub oscillator \, one octave \;   down \; noise 0..127 \, CC 33: noise level \; pwm 0..127 \, CC 34: pulse width: the depth of the LFO \, or the \;   width itself with pwmmode MAN \; pwmmode 0..1 \, CC 35: pulse width from the LFO or fixed \; dcolfo 0..127 \, CC 36: LFO to pitch (vibrato) \; lforate 0..127 \, CC 37: LFO rate \, 0.3 to 20 Hz \; lfodelay 0..127 \, CC 39: time until the LFO sets in after the \;   first key of a phrase \; vcflfo 0..127 \, CC 40: LFO to cutoff \; kybd 0..127 \, CC 41: keyboard follow of the cutoff \; envpol 0..1 \, CC 42: polarity of the envelope on the cutoff \; hpf 0..3 \, CC 43: high-pass filter \, four positions \; level 0..127 \, CC 44: level of the patch \, in front of the \;   chorus \; vcamode 0..1 \, CC 45: the amplifier follows the envelope or the \;   gate \; volume 0..127 \, CC 46: master volume \, after the chorus \; tune -50..50 \, CC 47: master tune in cents \; bend 0..7 \, CC 48: bender range in semitones \; octave -2..2 \, CC 49: octave transposition of the keyboard \; vellevel 0..127 \, CC 50: velocity to level. 0 is the \;   instrument \, which has no velocity \; velcutoff 0..127 \, CC 51: velocity to cutoff \, a soft key is \;   darker \; model 0..1 \, CC 52: which Juno the patch plays on: the Juno-60 \, \;   or the Juno-106 as measured on Cherry Audio's DCO-106 \;   (cutoff and resonance \, contour times and depth \, LFO \, \;   high-pass \, chorus \, sub and noise levels \, pulse width \, VCA \;   level) \; calibrate 0..127 \, CC 54: how far the six voices drift apart \, \;   as on an instrument out of calibration: each voice its own \;   tuning \, cutoff \, resonance and level. 0 is six identical \;   voices \; chorusnoise 0..127 \, CC 55: hiss of the chorus lines \, heard \;   while the chorus is on. 0 is none, f 64;
